Baseline PBR shading, atmosphere and surface detail
==================================================

Scope and shared contracts
  Improve baseline physically based rendering with and without LumOn. Provide a physically based
  sky, optional height/depth-map tessellation and relief mapping, PBR liquids, an investigation of
  approximate underwater caustics, and volumetric clouds.
  Keep standalone PBR usable without LumOn resources or indirect-lighting production. Standalone
  environmental lighting should keep outdoor shadows readable without injecting sunlight through
  occluders or applying an indiscriminate brightness floor to enclosed spaces.
  These are implementation tasks; algorithm choices and performance targets require investigation.
  Reuse existing shader/resource owners, compiled SPIR-V contracts, demand loading and settings.
  Keep optional features bounded, configurable and independently disableable.
  This list owns baseline PBR material improvements. Surface Cache performance
  and geometry work remain in docs/LumOn.SurfaceCache.PerformanceAndMaterials.todo.

Tasks

  ☐ Optimize water rendering and composition performance. See docs/PBR.WaterPerformance.todo.
    - Remove recurring executable preparation, reduce redundant receiver/scattering work, and
      gate unnecessary background capture while preserving the established water rendering contracts.

  Material-definition decision
    Retain shared BRDFProperties schema and C# models for existing material-wide properties.
    Per-axis-face overrides were withdrawn: texture-to-material mappings already let different
    block-face textures use different properties and naturally follow block rotation.

  ☑ Establish baseline PBR ownership, lighting contracts and comparison cases through source analysis.
    - Audit terrain, entities, transparent surfaces, liquids, sky, fog, shadows and final composition
      through actual engine call sites. Identify remaining vanilla shading and hidden LumOn coupling.
    - Define shared linear-color, exposure, material, direct-light, environmental-light and shadow
      conventions. Preserve existing held-light/world-coordinate corrections and energy accounting.
    - Define outdoor sun/shade, enclosed interior, emissive/held-light, water and night comparison
      cases for LumOn on/off. Identify pass-cost boundaries separately from shader startup.
      Per user direction, this audit does not require live captures or measured timings.
    - Record ownership and dependency boundaries in a linked design document; keep render composition
      thin and place atmosphere, liquids and surface-detail policy in their respective owners.
    - Completed source/IL evidence: docs/PBR.BaselineShading.Contracts.md. Traced checked-out API
      and survival source, installed shaders, VintagestoryLib and VSEssentials renderer methods.
      Confirmed Opaque-before-OIT ordering, animated-entity and held-item paths, RGBA atlas upload,
      RGBA8 primary output, retained vertex lighting and the startup-disabled LumOn reference gap.

  ☑ Correct the material color and HDR boundaries identified by the baseline audit.
    - Separate unlit material color from vanilla vertex lighting before deferred BRDF evaluation.
      Decode color textures consistently with representative CPU colors; keep data textures linear.
    - Resolve RGBA16F composite to RGBA8 primary conversion deliberately, with an explicit shared
      exposure/display boundary rather than implicit clipping before vanilla final grading.
    - Preserve colormaps, cutouts, emission and point-light behavior; validate linear-color and
      single-application lighting contracts through focused tests.
    - Implemented for opaque terrain/topsoil: unlit linear material capture, scene-linear HDR
      composition and a separate unit-exposure Reinhard/sRGB display resolve. The legacy sky
      bypasses the resolve; entity/OIT/late paths remain owned by the following task.
    - Source contracts and focused subagent-run validation: docs/PBR.MaterialColorAndDisplay.md.
      Final terrain checks passed 12/12; HDR/display, point-light and restoration checks passed.
      No game launch or live appearance/performance verification was required or performed.

  ☑ Establish PBR coverage for animated entities and late/transparent draw paths.
    - Integrate entityanimated and first-person standard/entityanimated variants at their actual
      engine stages. Do not assume standard/instanced patches cover animated entities or late items.
    - Keep OIT reveal/accumulation output locations distinct from opaque normal/material outputs.
      Provide lighting for OIT and AfterOIT independently of the earlier opaque deferred composite;
      coordinate liquid-specific behavior with the PBR liquid task below.
    - Implemented stage/target-aware material capture for animated and generic opaque meshes;
      forward PBR for transparent terrain and late/first-person draws. OIT retains its attachment
      layout; offscreen inventory/GUI draws retain vanilla shading. Liquids retain native lighting
      until the dedicated liquid task; conflicting opaque G-buffer injection was removed.
    - Shared BRDF, per-draw view-space light handling, sunlight color, shadows, emission, thermal
      tint and transparent normal/POM behavior are covered by focused subagent-run validation.
      47 distinct checks passed across installed shader variants, numerical lighting, routing,
      terrain and deferred regressions. Review findings were fixed and revalidated.
      Evidence and remaining HDR/environment boundaries: docs/PBR.EntityAndLateCoverage.md.

  ☑ Separate LumOn-compatible and standalone PBR pipelines with automatic mode selection.
    - Select the intended pipeline from LumOn.Enabled before initial shader preparation. Share common
      PBR code while giving each path explicit variants/contracts and resource dependencies.
    - LumOn mode consumes its indirect illumination exactly once. Standalone mode uses an explicit
      ambient/environment approximation, with suitable diffuse and specular responses and occlusion,
      so disabling LumOn does not leave outdoor shadows pitch black.
    - Keep direct-sun shadow visibility intact; do not solve ambient lighting by weakening shadows.
      Bound environment leakage into enclosed spaces and document approximation limits.
    - Implement coherent enable/disable, reload and failure behavior without stale LumOn textures,
      unnecessary GI allocations or unintended intermediate shader generations.
      Replace the constructor-captured nullable buffer reference so startup-off/later-enable can
      consume the newly created LumOn manager; preserve resource ownership and disposal boundaries.
      - Test both pipelines, live switching, startup with LumOn disabled, interior/exterior behavior,
        material response and absence of double-counted ambient/GI. Preserve standalone point lights.
      - Implemented and source-reviewed; contracts, approximation limits and validation receipts:
        docs/PBR.LightingModes.md. All 64 distinct focused checks passed; 153 shader stages and
        387 variants rebuilt. No game launch or live appearance/performance claim.

  ☑ Implement a physically based sky and shared atmospheric lighting inputs.
    - Evaluate an atmospheric scattering approach with explicit Rayleigh/Mie scattering, absorption,
      sun transmittance and bounded integration or lookup tables. Define scale and parameter units.
    - Integrate engine time, sun direction, weather and altitude; define moon/night/stars behavior.
      Maintain consistent exposure, horizon transitions and fog/aerial-perspective composition.
    - Supply consistent sky/environment and solar inputs to standalone PBR and LumOn consumers;
      avoid rendering a new sky while retaining incompatible illumination from the old model.
    - Bound lookup generation/update work and invalidate on actual atmospheric changes. Validate
      daylight, sunrise/sunset, night, weather transitions and below/above-horizon cases.
    - Implemented: docs/PBR.Atmosphere.md records the single-scattering model, shared lighting
      contracts, bounded lookup publication, engine celestial behavior and approximation limits.
    - Validation: 111 distinct focused tests passed; 153 shader stages / 387 variants rebuilt.
      Warmed CPU lookup work measured approximately 0.95 ms per bounded update on this machine.
      Source and implementation review completed; no game launch or live appearance claim.

  ☐ Fully replace the base-game sky shader with a VGE-owned shader.
    - Implemented ownership: atmosphere publication supplies physical-model-derived compatibility
      lighting before ambient blending; an independently registered sky IRenderer consumes it.
      A lifecycle prefix suppresses the complete engine sky callback regardless of draw readiness.
      Atmospheric/frame publication is independent of drawing; no per-frame vanilla fallback remains. Shared frame/resource
      inputs have a separate adapter; legacy cloud lookup content remains a compatibility dependency.
      Contract and limits: docs/PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md.
    - Owned pbr_sky SPIR-V and typed inputs reuse the published atmosphere and primary framebuffer;
      the renderer owns a procedural fullscreen triangle and reconstructs camera-relative rays per pixel. Runtime always emits scene-linear HDR.
      The earlier draw-hook/shader-handoff validation is historical and does not validate the revised
      ownership. User-run visual acceptance remains required before completion.
      Evidence: docs/PBR.Atmosphere.md / Owned fullscreen submission.
    - Persistent ownership validation: Debug build passed; 265 regression tests passed with five
      opt-in measurement skips, no GPU-availability skips. See docs/PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md.
      Twilight/night compatibility mapping and legacy cloud appearance still require user review.
    - Reuse the existing atmospheric LUT generation, publication and shared lighting inputs;
      replace the engine sky program rather than extending its GLSL with further HDR patches.
    - Use the established VGE shader asset, typed binding, registration and SPIR-V build paths.
      Establish draw ownership while preserving engine sky geometry, render order and celestial
      ordering; avoid duplicate sky draws and keep resource orchestration thin.
    - Explicitly own twilight/star visibility alpha, horizon and liquid-depth masking, underwater
      tint, night vision, psychedelic effects, glow and G-buffer/SSAO outputs. Specify which effects
      remain spatial sky operations and which belong at the final display boundary.
    - Emit scene-linear HDR on prepared HDR frames, retaining the compatible legacy display route
      until the common scene handoff is ready. Preserve values above one; apply display conversion
      and SDR dithering once at the selected boundary. Keep UI/offscreen rendering separate.
    - Enable scene-HDR participation only after the owned program and bindings are
      verified. This task supports full-scene HDR activation but does not gate water Phase 2.
    - Preserve shader reload, resize, world teardown, failure handling and both PBR lighting modes.
      Remove superseded sky source patches when the owned shader assumes their responsibilities.
    - Validate LUT row/seam sampling, day/night alpha and celestial ordering, perception effects,
      above/underwater views, HDR/legacy output and lifecycle with focused subagent tests. Retain
      existing atmospheric-model tests; obtain user-run visual acceptance without launching the game.
    - Contracts: docs/PBR.Atmosphere.md, docs/PBR.SharedDisplay.md, docs/PBR.OutputDithering.md,
      docs/PBR.MaterialColorAndDisplay.md and docs/PBR.WaterRefraction.md.

